node_modules/
# pnpm relocates its content-addressable store next to node_modules when the
# default store dir is on a different filesystem - which is exactly the case in
# the dev container (bind-mounted workspace vs container home, task 046).
.pnpm-store/
dist/
# `scripts/build-images.mjs`'s default output directory (issue #342). A bare
# `pnpm qcms:build-images` writes three OCI directories here, so without this line the
# first local image build leaves gigabytes of untracked tree in the working copy - and
# work selection stops when the checkout is dirty, so it is not merely untidy.
dist-images/
.turbo/
coverage/
# Where Vitest 5 puts everything it generates that is not a coverage report:
# `.vitest/attachments/`, `.vitest/blob/`, `.vitest/index.html`, `.vitest/json/` and
# `.vitest/junit/` ("generated reports are now in the .vitest directory",
# https://vitest.dev/guide/migration). No committed configuration enables any of those
# reporters today, so nothing writes here during `pnpm verify`; a hand-run
# `vitest --reporter=junit` does. Ignored ahead of that rather than after it, because an
# untracked directory at the repository root is read as source by every gate that derives
# its corpus from `git ls-files --others` (`scripts/tracked-files.mjs`) and stops work
# selection, which needs a clean checkout.
.vitest/
.claude/worktrees/
# Claude Code's per-machine settings overlay. `.claude/settings.json` is the shared,
# tracked harness configuration and stays tracked; the `.local.json` beside it is one
# operator's own permission grants on one machine, so it is never shareable. It was
# neither tracked nor ignored, which left it showing in `git status` - and
# Work selection stops when the shared checkout is
# dirty, so a local permissions file could halt a run before step 1. `**/` because the
# same local overlay can appear in nested tooling directories.
**/.claude/settings.local.json
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.compose.example
*.log
.DS_Store
# Generated by the isolated full-stack browser-test workflow. This file holds the
# random local-only administrator credential printed by `pnpm docker:up`. It must
# stay ignored under whatever name the harness writes: it was committed once,
# with live values, the moment a rename outran this line.
.e2e-full-stack-credentials.json
test-results/
playwright-report/
blob-report/

# Next 16 writes `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` into each app directory at build time
# (`next/dist/server/lib/generate-agent-files.js`). They are generated, not authored,
# and their text carries em dashes that `check:no-em-dash` correctly refuses, so a
# `git add -A` after a browser run reds the gate with a file nobody wrote. Ignored
# rather than fought: deleting them only re-creates them on the next run.
#
# THIS IS A STOPGAP, NOT THE CHOSEN FIX. It is the subject of open issue #431, which
# awaits a Code Owner decision between (a) ignoring the generator's output, which is
# what this rule does, and (b) suppressing the generation itself. (b) is the more
# durable of the two, because ignoring leaves em-dash-bearing files on disk in a repo
# whose gate exists to keep them out: this rule hides them from `git status`, it does
# not remove them. #431 can replace it without touching anything else here.
#
# Landed on its own because the trap cost four separate sessions in one day, and the
# generated file's own text argues the reader into committing it.
apps/*/AGENTS.md
apps/*/CLAUDE.md

# Chromium profile directories a Lighthouse run drops in the repository root under
# WSL, where its Windows-style default user-data path becomes one directory whose
# NAME contains backslashes. Each is a throwaway browser profile of a few hundred
# files. Ignored because a `git add -A` otherwise sweeps the lot into a commit,
# which is exactly what happened once. The backslashes are escaped rather than
# quoted: gitignore has no quoting, and `\\` is how a literal one is written.
C:\\Users\\*

# Throwaway ESLint fixture trees `scripts/eslint-workspace.test.ts` writes at the
# repository root (issue #899). The test needs a file the root flat config reaches and
# whose nearest `package.json` is the root one, so the fixture cannot live in a system
# temp directory; it is removed after each run, and this entry only covers a run that
# died before its cleanup.
/.lint-cwd-fixture-*/
